\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.1}{\ignorespaces Elements of proof for a global warming. Panel A shows the change in global surface temperature relative to the average of 1850-1900, as reconstructed (1-2000) and observation data (1850-2020). Panel B is a zoom of panel A with observed and simulated data, using human \& natural and only natural factors \citep {masson-delmotte_summary_2021}. Panel C present the top ten of the warmer years in global average (source: \url {https://www.climatecentral.org/}). Panel D is the global air surface temperature yearly evolution of the three years of my PhD, compared to 1992, the year of the third Earth summit when the United Nations committed to protect the future generations (data from C3S/ECMWF, ERA5, Copernicus: \url {https://pulse.climate.copernicus.eu/}).}}{10}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.2}{\ignorespaces The "Keeling Curve": this graph illustrates the evolution of atmospheric \ce {CO2} concentration over the last two thousand years. Named after Charles D. Keeling who initiated the measurement project of continuous \ce {CO2} measurement at Mauna Loa observatory (Hawaii, U.S.) in 1958 \citep {keeling_atmospheric_2017}, the data is completed by ice core measurements \citep {rubino_revised_2019}. The industrial revolution is clearly highlighted by the dramatic increase that started in the late 18{th} century. Data can be found here: \url {https://keelingcurve.ucsd.edu/}.}}{12}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.3}{\ignorespaces Historical \ce {CO2} emissions by regions and activities (A), put into perspective with the evolution of primary energy consumption by source. Data from \textit {Our World in Data}: \url {https://ourworldindata.org/}.}}{14}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.4}{\ignorespaces Effective radiative forcing (A) and associated additional warming (B) for different atmospheric species, from 1750 to 2019. Panel C is the change in global temperature, at 10 and 100 years after a single year pulse of 2019 anthropogenic emissions. Graphics from \citet {intergovernmental_panel_on_climate_change_climate_2023}.}}{15}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.5}{\ignorespaces Trend from implemented policies (red line) compared to emissions scenarios that would allow to stay below 1.5°C (cyan line) or below 2.0°C (green line) of global warming. A scenario that takes into account the National Determined Contributions (NDCs) discussed during COP26 is also represented (dark blue line). The NDCs represent the free-of-result commitments made by countries to reduce GHG emissions and enhance resilience to climate change impacts. Graphics from \citet {intergovernmental_panel_on_climate_change_climate_2023}.}}{17}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.6}{\ignorespaces Graphical abstract of the key aspects of the IPCC Assessment Report 6, Technical Summary. The upper panels are temperature projections for the five SSPs and three ranges of ECS. The lower panels are the responses of two climate indexes (heat warning index and change in extreme rainfall) for three levels of warming (1.5°C, 2.0°C and 4°C). The association of upper and lower panels allows to catch a glimpse of the timeline for future global warming consequences. Graphics from \citet {intergovernmental_panel_on_climate_change_climate_2023}.}}{20}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.7}{\ignorespaces Photographies of different cloud types and phenomena. Cumulus congestus over Sorbonne University (Jussieu campus) (A). Kelvin-Helmholtz instabilities in cumulus fractus clouds under asperitas clouds at sunset (B). Formation of a cumulonimbus at the end of the day above Paris Pantheon (C). Multilayered sky with nimbostratus on the left, precipitation in the background, cirrus far above everything else (D). Newly formed cumulus fractus and humilis (E). Paraglider's-eye view of an Alpin morning sky; multiple layers of stratocumulus co-evolve with convective columns pushed by the valley breeze (F). Clear sky cumulus mediocris crowned by Kelvin-Helmholtz instabilities (G). Big Kelvin-Helmholtz wave in stratocumulus over Scottish Highland mountains (H). Alto cumulus field in front of the full moon (I). All the pictures are from the author.}}{23}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.8}{\ignorespaces Atmospheric stability by temperature gradient (B), and associated mechanical analogy (A).}}{27}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.9}{\ignorespaces Zonal mean of the cloud fraction for different types of clouds (panel A). Liquid clouds (red line), ice clouds (blue line), mixed-phase clouds (purple line) and multi-layers clouds (green line). Panel B shows the annual (black line), summer (red line) and winter (blue line) of the zonal mean cloud fraction for mixed-phase clouds. Figure adapted from \citet {matus_role_2017}}}{29}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.10}{\ignorespaces Heterogeneous ice nucleation. Panel A is a schematic of the formation of an ice embryo in contact of an INP. The contact angle $\theta $ and the surface tensions $\sigma _i$ of the different actors are represented (adapted from \citet {lohmann_introduction_2016}). Panel B is the energy barrier $\Delta G$ expressed in thermal units, function of the ice embryo radius, for homogeneous and heterogeneous ice nucleation. The curves maximum is the critical Gibbs free energy that, once reached allows the embryo to grow as an ice crystal.}}{32}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.11}{\ignorespaces Representation of theoretical droplet freezing rate by homogeneous nucleation (liquid to ice) versus temperature. The corresponding average time needed for an ice crystal to form is plotted on the second axis. In the vicinity of $-38$°C, the nucleation rate rapidly decreases with increasing temperature, while the average time before freezing dramatically increases.}}{33}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.12}{\ignorespaces Schematics of the different main aerosols effects on clouds and the radiative budget. Graphics from \citet {ipcc_AR4_2007}.}}{36}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.13}{\ignorespaces Effective radiative forcing for aerosols (sum of \ce {SO2}, organic carbon, black carbon and ammonia), from 1750 to 2019. Effect due to aerosol-cloud interactions (deep purple) is distinguished from aerosol-radiation effect (light purple). The uncertainties have reduced since the fifth IPCC Assessment report, but still remain important. Graphics from \citet {intergovernmental_panel_on_climate_change_climate_2023}.}}{37}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.14}{\ignorespaces Sea sprays are emitted during the bursting of fine water droplets projected by wind and waves. They contain sea salts and organic matter from marine life.}}{39}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.15}{\ignorespaces One year (2012-2013) time series of INP concentrations active at different temperatures. Panel A shows the raw concentrations alongside polynomial fits. The magnitude of the seasonal cycle varies with the activation temperature. Panel B is the averaged Arctic temperature and precipitation on the same period. Data from \citet {wex_annual_2019}.}}{42}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.16}{\ignorespaces Map of the Arctic, illustrating the different definitions of the region frontiers and its main biomes. The dashed and solid blue lines are the ice edge of respectively the annual minimum and maximum median sea ice extent. The map is adapted from \citet {maillard_boundary_2022}.}}{44}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.17}{\ignorespaces Annual mean surface temperature anomalies for the period 2004\--2024, relative to the 1951--1980 mean. The zonal mean anomalies are shown in the lower right panel. Warming reaches up to \SI {3}{\degreeCelsius } in the Arctic, approximately four times the magnitude observed at the equator. Data from \url {https://data.giss.nasa.gov/gistemp/maps/NASA GISS}}}{46}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.18}{\ignorespaces Climatology of sea level pressure (SLP) for the period 1979--2021, averaged over 50--90\degree N. On average, the Arctic exhibits a high-pressure anomaly, consistent with a negative phase of the Arctic Oscillation (AO). Data from \citet {copernicus_climate_change_service_era5_2017}.}}{47}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.19}{\ignorespaces Illustrations of Arctic amplification feedbacks. Panel A is a schematics of the sea ice albedo feedback principle. First, a positive forcing induces an increase in temperature. This causes the melting of sea ice and snow, which leads to a decrease in albedo. Short wave radiation is then more strongly absorbed by the surface, which amplifies the temperature increase induced by the initial forcing, closing the positive feedback loop. Panel B: schematics of the lapse-rate feedback effect. The inversion of the vertical temperature profile induced, in the case of a surface warming of $\Delta T_{\text {surf}}$, a negative buoyancy \textbf {B} below the inversion altitude. The heat gain is then poorly transported to the upper layers of the atmosphere, and the warming $\Delta T_{TOA}$ at the tropopause is minimal.}}{48}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.20}{\ignorespaces Zonal mean of the cloud radiative effect on the surface for different types of clouds (panel A). Liquid clouds (red line), ice clouds (blue line), mixed-phase clouds (purple line) and multi-layers clouds (green line). Panel B shows the annual (black line), summer (red line) and winter (blue line) of the zonal mean CRE of mixed-phase clouds. Figure adapted from \citet {matus_role_2017}}}{50}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {I.21}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the general question: Is there a feedback loop in the INP emissions, aerosol-cloud interactions and Arctic radiative budget causal relationships, and what would be its sign?}}{52}{figure.caption.37}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the tracer emissions in WRF-Chem and concentration series reconstructions. Regions of emissions of sea ice \textbf {(a)}, open ocean \textbf {(b)}, and continental \textbf {(c)} tracers. \textbf {(d)} Example of reconstructed series of daily concentrations interpolated at Ny-Ålesund. \textbf {(e)} Map of the Arctic with the locations of the five studied stations. Base map from Cartopy {\copyright }~British Crown copyright 2016.}}{71}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.2}{\ignorespaces Application of the ratio method as it is depicted in \cref {P1sec:ratio_method}. Panel \textbf {(a)} shows the reconstructed daily concentration series of the continental tracer interpolated at Ny-Ålesund over the 2 years of simulation. Panel \textbf {(b)} shows the climatology of the total air mass origins; panel \textbf {(c)} shows the climatology of the origins of air masses that brought the 10\,{\%} highest concentrations; and panel \textbf {(d)} shows the ratio of \textbf {(c)} to \textbf {(b)}, noted as $R_\text {h}^{10}$.}}{77}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.3}{\ignorespaces Application of the improved ratio method. Similarly to \cref {P1fig:basic_method}, \textbf {(a)} shows the reconstructed daily concentration series of the continental tracer interpolated at Ny-Ålesund with the comparison of the standard sorting (top) and the seasonal sorting (bottom). Panel \textbf {(b)} is the climatology of the total air mass origins; \textbf {(c)} is the climatology of the origins of air masses that brought the 10\,{\%} highest concentrations; and \textbf {(d)} is the ratio of \textbf {(c)} to \textbf {(b)}, noted as $R_\text {h}^{10}$. In \textbf {(b)} and \textbf {(c)}, the 2\,{\%} lowest FPES values have been removed, as explained in \cref {P1sec:improved_method}.}}{79}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.4}{\ignorespaces Comparison of the results obtained with the standard ratio method \textbf {(a)} and with the improved ratio method \textbf {(b)}. Every panel presents the detection results for a specific tracer and for the five stations. The bars represent the contributions of each surface type to the detection (light blue for sea ice, blue for open ocean, green for continent). For every pair of tracer and station, the level of success is shown above the corresponding bars.}}{81}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.5}{\ignorespaces Results of the combination of the five stations. Panel \textbf {(a)} shows the fraction of the contribution of every surface type for the three tracers and the associated success level. Panels \textbf {(b)}, \textbf {(c)}, and \textbf {(d)} are the composite ratio maps for the sea ice tracer, the open ocean tracer, and the continental tracer, respectively.}}{84}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.6}{\ignorespaces Identification of the origin of the sea-ice tracer at Tiski station by 3 different methods. Panels \textbf {(a)} and \textbf {(b)} are the climatologies of, respectively, the total FPES value and the values associated with the 10\,{\%} highest observations ($S_\text {h}^{10}$). Panels \textbf {(c)} and \textbf {(d)} are the ratio maps of the two versions of the ratio method ($R_\text {h}^{10}$ for, respectively, the standard and improved versions). Panel \textbf {(e)} is the quantification of the detection results for the climatology associated with the highest observation and for both versions of the ratio method.}}{86}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.7}{\ignorespaces Evolution of the success level sum for different levels of low FPES filtering. In \textbf {(a)}, the sum is performed based on the scores of the five studied stations (Alert, Ny-Ålesund, Tiksi, Utqiaġvik, Villum) independently for the three tracers (sea ice, open ocean, continent). The total success level is shown as a black line. Panel \textbf {(b)} takes only the success levels superior or equal to 2 and sets them to 1. Dashed lines represent the theoretical maxima of the individual tracer scores (gray) and of the total score (black).}}{88}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.8}{\ignorespaces Results of the improved ratio method when applied to a data series of absorption coefficients measured at the Zeppelin Observatory (Ny-Ålesund, Svalbard). Panel \textbf {(a)} is the ratio of the improved method that highlights the likely regions of origin. Panel \textbf {(b)} is the quantification of the surface type contributions to the three sources (sea ice, open ocean, continent).}}{90}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.9}{\ignorespaces Results of the improved ratio method when applied to the data series of methanesulfonic acid (MSA) measured at Zeppelin Observatory. Panel \textbf {(a)} is the measurement series of MSA over 2020. The points corresponding to the 10\,{\%} strongest anomalies are represented in orange (high anomaly) and light-blue (low anomaly) squares. Panels \textbf {(b)} and \textbf {(c)} show the results for the complete series of measurements (January to December~2020). Panels \textbf {(d)} and \textbf {(e)} show the results for a period of continuous measurements of the dataset (April to early July).}}{95}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.10}{\ignorespaces Identification of the source origins of the INPs active at \SI {-20}{\degreeCelsius } and observed during the MOCCHA campaign. The detection uses the improved ratio method. Panel A is the raw ratio of the \SI {10}{\percent } highest observed concentrations. Panel B is the raw ratio of the \SI {33}{\percent } lowest observed concentrations. Yellow to red indicate detection signal, while the blueish colour means no signal. Panel C is the composite ratio (c.f. \cref {P1sec:improved_method}). Panel D is the time series of INP concentrations measured during the campaign for four activation temperatures.}}{97}{figure.caption.59}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.11}{\ignorespaces Identification of the source origins of the PBAPS observed during the MOCCHA campaign, using the improved ratio method. Panel A is the origin of air masses outside the events identified in Panel B. Panel B is the time series of the PBAP measurements. Panel C is the origin of air masses between the peaks. Panel D and E are the composite ratios for peak one and two respectively. The signal is in shade of red. The blue colour depicts the sea ice concentration.}}{99}{figure.caption.60}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.12}{\ignorespaces Emission sources of MOSAIC INPs detected with the improved ratio method. Panel A shows the composite ratio maps corresponding to four INP series, for activation temperature from \SI {-10}{\degreeCelsius } to \SI {-25}{\degreeCelsius }. Panel B is the time series of the corresponding INP concentrations during the MOSAIC campaign, from November 2019 to March 2020.}}{102}{figure.caption.61}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.13}{\ignorespaces Same as \cref {fig:source_INP_moccha} for the ARTofMELT campaign. The INP series used for the detection is the \SI {-20}{\degreeCelsius } one. Panel A is the raw ratio of the \SI {10}{\percent } highest observed concentrations. Panel B is the raw ratio of the \SI {33}{\percent } lowest observed concentrations. Yellow to red indicate detection signal, while the blueish colour means no signal. Panel C is the composite ratio (c.f. \cref {P1sec:improved_method}), the actual identification results. Panel D is the time series of INP concentrations measured during the campaign for four activation temperatures.}}{103}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.14}{\ignorespaces Panel A is the total burned area during June–October derived from MODIS observations, for year 2018. Panel B is the time series of total column \ce {CO} concentrations observed by IASI over Paris' coordinates.}}{105}{figure.caption.63}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.15}{\ignorespaces Composite ratio maps computed on the PES \SIrange {4000}{\metre } for different starting altitudes. It shows the likely source of the Paris high \ce {CO} event of the end of August 2018. Each map shows the results for a different starting altitude of the FLEXPART-WRF tracers emission, from \SI {5}{\km } to \SI {8}{\km }. The interpretation of the maps enables the detection of the \ce {CO} plume altitude when it passed over Paris.}}{106}{figure.caption.64}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.16}{\ignorespaces Time series of total \ce {CO} column for Paris, New York and over the Atlantic (A). Results of the improved ratio method for the combinaison of the three locations source tracing (B).}}{107}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {III.17}{\ignorespaces Statistical distributions of the continental contribution to air masses origins during the four airborne measurement campaigns.}}{109}{figure.caption.67}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.1}{\ignorespaces Averaged emission fluxes of modelled coarse-mode aerosol particles (dust, MPOA, bacteria and fungal spores) over the month of August 2018, during the MOCCHA 2018 campaign.}}{118}{figure.caption.73}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.2}{\ignorespaces INP number concentration spectrum of the MOCCHA 2018 measurements.}}{119}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.3}{\ignorespaces \todo {to be updated, and changed to activation fraction} | Activation fraction depending on temperature for the tested parametrisations.}}{120}{figure.caption.77}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.4}{\ignorespaces Evolution of the activation fraction of three INAS parametrisations, against temperature and particle diameter in semi-logarithmic scale. The $\gamma $ factor is not yet applied here, so all reach \SI {100}{\percent } activation. The inclinaison of the threshold lines accounts for the relative weight of particle diameter and temperature for the activation fraction. The saturated darker zones show the parameter space for which the parametrisations predict between \SI {10}{\percent } and full activation. Panel A is H18B (bacteria), B is M18 (sea sprays) and C is N12 (dust).}}{122}{figure.caption.81}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.5}{\ignorespaces Mass concentrations of modelled coarse aerosols particles from WRF-Chem compared to the coarse aerosols observations during the MOCCHA campaign. Panel A shows the concentrations of the five size bins of the GOCART dust particles. Panel B is the concentrations series of modelled PBAPs, including bacteria, fungal spores and MPOA. Panel C compares the sum of all the size bins and species of the WRF-Chem particles with the observations of coarse mode particles from the MOCCHA campaign.}}{125}{figure.caption.84}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.6}{\ignorespaces Modelled compared to observed INP concentrations at three activation temperatures. Panel A shows concentrations obtained with the modelled aerosols particles. Panel B shows the results obtained with the observed particles as input for the parametrisations. The INP observations series from the MOCCHA campaign 2018 are plotted in black dashed-line and red crosses.}}{128}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.7}{\ignorespaces Concentrations of modelled INPs for four activation temperatures, compared to observation data from the MOCCHA campaign.}}{129}{figure.caption.87}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.8}{\ignorespaces Maps of modelled INP concentrations for four activation temperatures. Panel A shows an event of continental particles transport toward central Arctic, and panel B is the average INP concentrations over the month of August 2018 (MOCCHA campaign period). Colour bars are centred on $10^{-1}$ INP per liter; blue areas indicate values lower than this threshold, while red areas means concentrations greater than it.}}{130}{figure.caption.88}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.9}{\ignorespaces Similar to \cref {fig:INP_concs_MOCCHA} for MOSAIC. Concentrations of modelled INPs for four activation temperatures, compared to observation data from the MOSAIC campaign.}}{132}{figure.caption.89}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {IV.10}{\ignorespaces Same as \cref {fig:icenuc_params_moccha} B but focused on organic particles. The fPBAP measurement series is plotted along with the observed and modelled INP concentrations.}}{135}{figure.caption.90}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces Ratio maps of the fifteen cases used in the tracer experiment. Each column corresponds to one a the five Arctic stations, and each row to one of the three tracers. The maps show the regions of emission identified by the standard ratio method. One can easily notice that the identified regions do not stick to the emission regions of the tracers.}}{143}{figure.caption.98}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Same as Fig. \ref {fig:sup_standard_allstations} but for the improved ratio method. Composite ratio maps of the fifteen cases used in the tracer experiment. Each column corresponds to one a the five Arctic stations, and each row to one of the three tracers. The maps show the regions of emission identified by the improved ratio method that benefited of the three modifications suggested in Sect. 3.3: background subtraction, FPES filtering, and composite ratio.}}{144}{figure.caption.99}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces Comparison of the results obtained with different combinations of modifications to the standard ratio method. (a) is background subtraction and composite ratio, (b) is background subtraction alone, (c) is composite ratio and FPES filtering, (d) is composite ratio alone, (e) is FPES filtering and background subtraction, (f) is FPES filtering alone. Every panel presents the detection results for a specific tracer and for the five stations. The bars represent the contributions of each surface type to the detection (light blue for sea ice, blue for open ocean, green for continent). For every pair of tracer and station, the level of success is showed above the corresponding bars. The averaged result of each case is reported in Table 4.}}{145}{figure.caption.100}%
